import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'/home/claude/work'); from gen import *
W='/tmp/tlv-src/apps/web'; A='https://apocalypse12.com'
make(21,f"This is a summary. The Fathers on chapter 21 are set out in full on The Woman and the Ark: [The Bride, the Wife of the Lamb]({A}/the-bride-the-wife-of-the-lamb) and [The City, the Temple and the Garden]({A}/the-city-the-temple-and-the-garden), which gives the full table of the twelve stones.",
[(f'{A}/the-bride-the-wife-of-the-lamb','The Bride, the Wife of the Lamb (The Woman and the Ark)'),(f'{A}/the-city-the-temple-and-the-garden','The City, the Temple and the Garden (The Woman and the Ark)')],
[('21:1–4','A new heaven and a new earth',[
 ('Renewed, not replaced.','On this the Fathers cited give one answer. Oecumenius reads the verse with St. Paul: creation will be set free from its slavery to corruption (Romans 8:21). St. Augustine: what burns away is the corruptible quality of things, so that a world made new may suit bodies made new. Cassiodorus: a man is still himself when he rises, and so are heaven and earth.'),
 ('The new Jerusalem coming down.','Augustine: she comes down from heaven because God makes her by heavenly grace, and her descent began with time itself, as her citizens are born through the bath of rebirth. Apringius of Beja: the city is the multitude of the saints.'),
 ('No more tears.','Tertullian argues from causes: where could sorrow and death come from in the presence of God, with the devil cast out? Primasius adds a point for now: here, the holier a person is, the more he weeps in prayer.'),
]),
('21:5–9','“I make all things new”',[
 ('The Alpha and the Omega.','St. Andrew of Caesarea: first by his divinity and last by his humanity, and his care reaches from the first creature to the last.'),
 ('A gift.','Oecumenius asks how the water can be free when the saints reach it through such hardship. It is free because no labour is worth what is given (Romans 8:18).'),
]),
('21:10–21','The city, its gates and foundations',[
 ('The wall and the gates.','The wall is Christ, all say. The gates are the apostles, through whom we came to the faith; Apringius adds that Christ alone is the door (John 10:9). Three gates on each side: the four quarters of the world have received faith in the Trinity.'),
 ('Twelve foundations, one foundation.','Primasius explains how there can be twelve when St. Paul says there is one (1 Corinthians 3:11): the apostles are foundations in Christ, as they are the light of the world in him.'),
 ('The stones and the pearls.','Andrew assigns each stone to an apostle; St. Bede reads each as a grace of the Christian life. Each gate is a single pearl, and the one pearl is Christ, from whom the apostles have their brightness.'),
]),
('21:22–27','No temple, no night',[
 ('No temple.','Apringius: a temple was given so that people might reach by faith what cannot be seen. Where God shows himself openly, none is wanted. Oecumenius: we know now as in a mirror, then face to face (1 Corinthians 13:12).'),
 ('Nothing unclean.','Apringius: no one enters who refuses to be cleansed of sin, or who, once cleansed and soiled again, refuses the washing of humility and tears.'),
]),
],f'{W}/src/data/fathers/chapter-21.tsx'); wire(21,W)
make(22,f"This is a summary. The Fathers on chapter 22 are set out in full on The Woman and the Ark, in the later sections of [The City, the Temple and the Garden]({A}/the-city-the-temple-and-the-garden), which ends with the book’s last words, “Come, Lord Jesus.”",
[('/topics/revelation-and-the-mass','Revelation and the Mass'),(f'{A}/the-city-the-temple-and-the-garden','The City, the Temple and the Garden (The Woman and the Ark)')],
[('22:1–2','The river and the tree of life',[
 ('The river.','Apringius of Beja: Baptism, in this life; the living water is the Lord himself (John 4:10). St. Andrew of Caesarea: in the Jerusalem above, the Holy Spirit, flowing from the Father through the Son. Primasius: Christ, “the Fountain who is also the River.” St. Jerome notes that there is one throne, for the kingship of the Trinity is one.'),
 ('The tree.','Jerome: Christ, planted on both banks, which are the Old Testament and the New. Oecumenius: Wisdom (Proverbs 3:18). St. Caesarius of Arles: no tree bears fruit in every season except the cross of the Lord.'),
]),
('22:6–11','“I am coming soon”',[
 ('“Soon.”','No Father cited computes a date. Oecumenius: all time is short beside the ages to come. Andrew: the shortness of the present age, or the swiftness of each person’s death.'),
 ('“Worship God.”','St. Athanasius draws the conclusion from the angel’s refusal: a creature does not worship a creature. Worship belongs to God alone.'),
 ('“Do not seal up the words.”','The book is to be read by all the faithful. Caesarius adds that the Scriptures stay sealed to the proud and open to the humble.'),
]),
('22:12–16','The recompense; the morning star',[
 ('“I bring with me the recompense.”','St. Clement of Rome, about the year 96, already echoes it: we must be eager to do good, for the Lord is coming with his reward (*1 Clement* 34).'),
 ('“Blessed are they who wash their robes.”','Apringius: washed in the blood of the Lord’s Passion at Baptism, and afterward by the tears of repentance.'),
 ('Root and offspring of David.','Root as God, the cause of David and of all things, and offspring as man, born of David’s line: one Christ in two natures (Oecumenius, St. Bede).'),
]),
('22:17–21','“Come, Lord Jesus”',[
 ('The Spirit and the bride say, “Come.”','Oecumenius and Andrew hear the Church and the Spirit asking for the coming of the Son. It is the petition “your kingdom come,” and only someone trying to live justly can pray it honestly.'),
 ('A gift.','Primasius: even the will to come is a gift of grace, for no one comes unless the Father draws him (John 6:44).'),
]),
],f'{W}/src/data/fathers/chapter-22.tsx'); wire(22,W)
